--TEST--
A method declared `: never` lowers whether or not it is called, and a call to one does not come back: the statement after it is never reached and the frame leaves through the enclosing `catch` — `rule:types/grammar`, `rule:errors/propagation`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/void-never-self-static
// `rule:types/grammar` makes `never` a return-only atom, and it is the one atom whose
// value question has no value in it: a frame that cannot come back hands its
// caller nothing. `nvs_ir::lower::erase_checked_ty` gives it `void`'s
// representation for that reason, and that arm owns the rest of the decision.
class Fail {
    // Never called anywhere below. The *declaration* is what reached the
    // lowerer, and it had no arm for this type — findings.md § Panics P4.
    public static function unused(): never {
        throw new RuntimeError("unused");
    }

    public static function with(string $why): never {
        throw new RuntimeError($why);
    }
}

class Work {
    public static function run(bool $ok): void {
        echo "entered\n";
        if (!$ok) {
            // The terminator is the callee's own, not a mark on this site: the
            // `throw` inside `with` leaves the frame, and `rule:errors/propagation`'s unwind
            // edge carries it past `run` to the `catch` below. So the `echo`
            // after this call is lowered like any other statement and is
            // reached by nothing.
            Fail::with("refused");
        }
        echo "finished\n";
    }
}

echo "compiled\n";

Work::run(true);

try {
    Work::run(false);
    echo "the call came back\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo "caught: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
compiled
entered
finished
entered
caught: refused
